  "lessons": [
    {
      "title": "Name a routine",
      "skill": "Your cards",
      "body": "Study Stairs to the nest. Stair step is a Your card: four printed cards saved under one number. Write out every card that Repeat 4, Stair step, End repeat stands for, and count how many cards it saves.",
      "ask": "What makes a run of cards worth saving as a Your card?"
    },
    {
      "title": "Write a level for another group",
      "skill": "Authoring",
      "body": "Each group draws a six by six board on squared paper: a start square and facing, a goal, up to six walls, one or two gates, and a finish card if you like. Write a row that solves it and count its cards. That count is your par. Trade boards with another group and play theirs.",
      "ask": "What did the other group find hard about your level that you did not expect?"
    },
    {
      "title": "Prove your par",
      "skill": "Checking par",
      "body": "Kick from the island was first written with par 9; another group solved it with 4 by letting the stairs keep going. Before trading a level, try hard to beat your own par. If someone else beats it, the par was wrong, not the players, so change the number.",
      "ask": "How could you be sure no shorter row exists?"
    },
    {
      "title": "Steps par by hand",
      "skill": "Search",
      "body": "A steps par can be found for certain: from the start, mark every square the duck can reach in one step, then two, then three, like ripples on a pond, until the goal is marked. Do it for one level on paper, then compare with the par the pack's checker computed.",
      "ask": "Why is a steps par easier to be sure of than a cards par?"
    },
    {
      "title": "When it falls",
      "skill": "Events",
      "body": "Add a When it falls row to your solution: what the duck does once it is back on its feet. The board does not play it, but a duck running your row would. Test it by having the student playing the duck sit down when you say fall.",
      "ask": "Should the duck carry on after a fall, or go back to the start?"
    }
  ],
